Property values within 500m of rapid transit stations are 10–25% higher than comparable properties further away — transit investment creates real estate value that can finance the investment.
A fully employed minimum-wage worker without a car spends 15–30% of income on transportation in US cities; high-quality transit reduces this to 5–8%, a significant poverty-reduction effect.
New York City's transit system enables an economy of $2 trillion GDP in a 468 sq mile footprint — density-dependent economic activity that would be impossible with car-only infrastructure.
